Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:08.950 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello everyone my name is nori and today  i'd like to go over some ways to  practice arpeggios that i find to be the  most effective in general an arpeggio is  a form of broken chord where the lines  are either ascending or descending in  the span of either an octave or more so  something like this  or  or something like this  [Music]  these are all arpeggios so the way i  would practice arpeggios is pretty  similar to the way i would practice any  other line formation but there are some  specifics the first and most common way  for me to practice these is to apply  rhythm and accents now i made a brief  practice notes video a couple months  back outlining the different concepts  behind rhythmic practicing so if you  want to check that out i'll include a  link so let me just go over a few  so for this example let's look at part  of chopin's opus 10 number one starting  from measure 29.  so in my opinion these measures are one  of the most difficult in this etude and  it's because the hand positions are very  awkward and they're spread out so here  are a few ways that we would apply  rhythm  [Music]  so  [Music]  you can also apply triplets  [Music]  and when we're doing this we can shift  where we're starting our dotted note  rhythm so that we can cover all the  bases  [Music]  we can also add emphasis to the notes  that we've been holding with accenting  and again we can get creative with the  types of rhythms and the groupings of  accents that we're using now there are a  few reasons why practicing with rhythms  and accents is very effective  the first reason is that it allows us to  pay attention to certain notes that are  somewhat neglected when we're just  playing it straight this allows us to  strengthen those fingers used to play  those notes because we're emphasizing  and exaggerating the use of those  fingers the second reason is specific to  rhythmic practicing because it allows us  to build up towards a faster tempo by  working on small groups and chunks of  notes and what we're doing is we're  playing these small groups of notes very  fast  another reason is somewhat of a casual  thought but it's still quite important  to me  and it's that practicing in this way  keeps things a little more interesting  because there's a lot of variety when  i'm repeating the same thing over and  over again there are different patterns  to apply different types of accents and  i can get creative with it so that to me  is important and i think it's worth  mentioning  so going back to this passage  [Music]  it's useful to organize these arpeggios  into different hand positions  so let's go through them one by one the  first one  now  unless you rock mod enough that whole  stretch  is not a hand position  so to me i would break it up into two  different ways so this leads to another  common way of practicing these which is  to block certain notes  so in this case i would block  or  [Music]  and i organize in this way just by the  natural positioning of the hands now  let's look at the next measure  so here  it's very awkward  i would use fingers five four two one  because  to stretch a fifth with fingers two and  three is a bit much  [Music]  i would organize this into e flat  e flat  [Music]  the next one  [Music]  for this one i would really only stick  with one way of organizing the notes  which is  c and e flat and a and e flat  [Music]  there's no use in trying to block these  wide cords which you would use 2 3 and 5  with  it's not really productive so i would  just stick with this  and of course we're practicing this in  this blocking style  [Music]  but as we're doing this we're getting  used to adjusting slightly between these  different groups  [Music]  and in that period of adjustment we're  also relaxed and we're able to find our  next grip  so the more we do this the faster we'll  be able to do this eventually and the  faster we'll be adjusting our hand  positions between  the different groups  so the next one also  [Music]  in that group and also  is reasonable here's another note  specific to this chord  but could be applied in other areas as  well so when we're going from  this position  to reach over to the d  if we don't move our elbows towards the  right  the d is quite a bit of a stretch from  the f and a flat  and that way  it's hard to be accurate again as a  reason to why organizing the hand  positions is important is that in this  case  if  the two different positions are very  clear to you  and in this case two notes are  overlapping  you know what to do with your elbow  and you're prepared to get these fingers  out of the way when we're playing the d  so the thing that we need to become  comfortable with is transitioning from  this position  to the next position  and in doing that it's basically two  motions scooping away from the keys and  towards the right to reach the d  because if we move just towards the  right this black key will get in the way  and if we just move away from the keys  we're not reaching the d so i know this  is basically common sense but it's very  useful to be aware of these very very  fundamental and obvious things  because when we're not thinking about it  sometimes we're not really sure why  we're missing certain notes or why  certain patterns are very difficult  so to just finish off the section with  this a major chord  in this particular instance i would  group it so that  this fourth is the anchor  [Music]  and in the pattern like this it really  applies to most of these chords  especially in this etude  the middle two notes are very often the  anchor points in this pattern so when  we're working on techniques such as  practicing arpeggios in this case it's  also a great opportunity to work on some  harmonic concepts at the same time  that's partially why and how i came up  with the exercises from my book of  modified chopin exercises where each one  takes you through a specific type of  technique as well as some harmonic  progressions scales and or modes if  you're interested in getting a copy i'll  include a link in the description  [Music]  i'm going to call this one  teeter-tottering before i used to call  this oscillating notes  basically i'm repeating groups of two  notes as i'm going up or down the  arpeggio  [Music]  so again like with practicing with  rhythms and accents we can get quite  creative with the types of patterns and  formations we're using with this type of  [Music]  teeter-tottering  [Music]  a great way to turn our ears and our  fingers to play more evenly is to start  off something like this with  a very exaggerated length of one of  these  teeter-totters  i find that in doing this it really  primes our fingers and our ears  to  be very sensitive to things being even  we can also choose to repeat certain  notes  [Music]  and again there are a ton of variations  to this we can choose to repeat every  other note or every group of three  so on and so forth  [Music]  so i hope you found those tips helpful  please subscribe if you haven't already  and i'll see you in the next video  [Music]  you

